<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
  <title>Reading Quiz 04: Newton's laws</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Contents:</alert> This quiz covers sections 3.1 through 3.5
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Instructions:</alert> Answer all the questions to the best of your ability and upload your work to the 
      <q>Quiz 4</q> assignment in Canvas.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
            The ISS is at an altitude (distance from the surface of the Earth) of about 400km. Calculate the ratio of
            the force of gravity felt by an astronaut at the ISS to the force of gravity they feel on Earth's surface.
            Given the result, can you explain why astronauts float in the space station?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	 Expand on the result of example 3.2. While still taking the rope to be massive (but totally horizontal),
             find the tension at the rope's center. Don't start from scratch: use the results
             from that problem.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          		Physically, why should the tension <m>T(x)</m> from example 3.3 approach 0 as <m>~{x\to 0}</m>?
                Your answer doesn't have to be a long paragraph, but make sure you are saying more than
                 <q>because it has to be</q>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider the statement that <q>friction opposes motion</q>. Come up with an example where the friction
                force on an object is in the direction of motion, <q>propelling</q> the object forward. What motion 
                would you say that friction is really opposing, then? Check page 92 for help. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

  </page>
</worksheet>